abstract: >
  Computer-assisted numerical evidence for a strict local maximum of the
  moving-sofa area functional, on the Sobolev space H^2 of corner
  trajectories (modulo translation symmetry). The headline new
  contribution is the first quantitative local-maximality evidence for
  Romik's ambidextrous candidate Sigma (the second half of Romik's 2018
  Open Problem 1), which is implied by no prior work; the identical
  apparatus is also developed on Gerver's sofa, whose global (hence
  local) optimality is in any case established by Baek (2024). We stress
  that these are NOT theorems in the strict computer-assisted-proof
  sense: they are conditional on three explicitly identified
  non-rigorous ingredients, flagged throughout. Conditional on these,
  the Gerver evidence yields a coercivity constant m >= 4.27 in the
  H^2-norm and a uniqueness radius delta >= 0.044; the ambidextrous
  evidence yields m^R >= 2.2. The three ingredients are: (i) the
  finite-dimensional Hessian is evaluated with a floating-point
  polygon-intersection oracle (Shapely) rather than true interval
  arithmetic; (ii) the cross-term coupling between low and high Fourier
  modes is bounded by a numerical placeholder rather than an analytic
  estimate; (iii) the second variation Q decomposes as Q_smooth +
  Q_jump, where Lemma 8 characterizes Q_smooth analytically but the
  dominant jump piece Q_jump (from the four contact-transition
  breakpoints, whose locations move under perturbation) is captured only
  empirically; a closed-form derivation of Q_jump is identified as the
  principal remaining analytic item. v0.3.0 reframes the work from
  "proof/theorem/certified" to honest conditional numerical evidence,
  foregrounds the ambidextrous result, adds explicit Sobolev-space
  definitions and a coercivity-implies-maximality discussion, assumes
  Baek's global resolution throughout, and reconciles the constants
  (m >= 4.27, tail <= 0.28, delta >= 0.044).
keywords:
  - moving sofa problem
  - Gerver's sofa
  - ambidextrous sofa
  - Romik
  - local maximality
  - second variation
  - computer-assisted
